00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:52.350
The model development cycle
is more or less like this.

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:53.970
You have a problem to solve.

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:57.150
You collect data that will
help you solve that problem.

00:05:57.150 --> 00:05:59.499
You almost always have
to clean the data.

00:05:59.499 --> 00:06:01.290
Then you decide what
model you want to use.

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:03.930
If it's a image classification
or some sort of perception

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:08.310
problem, audio, maybe some text,
you might use a neural network.

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:10.284
But if it's a clickthrough
rate prediction,

00:06:10.284 --> 00:06:11.700
you might use
logistic regression.

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:13.408
If it's structured
learning, maybe you'll

00:06:13.408 --> 00:06:16.110
use gradient-boosted decision
tree, something to that effect.

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:17.776
Those of you that
have been in the field

00:06:17.776 --> 00:06:21.210
long enough kind of already
know this model works well

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:22.480
with this type of problem.

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:24.688
Then you need me to go
through and take this raw data

00:06:24.688 --> 00:06:27.207
that you've cleaned, and
analyzed, and collected,

00:06:27.207 --> 00:06:29.040
and you need to do some
feature engineering.

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:31.164
Especially if it's logistic
regression or some sort

00:06:31.164 --> 00:06:33.960
of linear model, you're gonna
need possibly quantization,

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:35.310
one-hot encoding.

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:38.580
You're going to need to do
feature crosses, all sorts

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:41.310
of logs of certain
types of features,

00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:43.050
all those types of things.

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:45.480
And once you've done that,
you can now take your data set

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:46.860
and you can train a model.

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:49.870
And most models have
hyper-parameters.

00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:51.690
And your goal is
not to get a model,

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:54.690
it's to get the best possible
model given the model

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:56.996
selection and the feature set.

00:06:56.996 --> 00:06:57.870
So you'll train that.

00:06:57.870 --> 00:06:59.410
You'll do some
hyper-parameter tuning,

00:06:59.410 --> 00:07:01.243
and at the end of the
day, you have a model.

00:07:01.243 --> 00:07:03.720
And you'll take that model and
you'll analyze the results.

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:05.740
Hey, what's this
model doing well?

00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:07.560
Where does it succeed?

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:08.460
Where does it fail?

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:10.690
And what can I do to get
a better model out of it?

00:07:10.690 --> 00:07:12.189
And depending on
what the errors are

00:07:12.189 --> 00:07:15.240
that you see, you may decide,
ah, I didn't have enough data.

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:17.130
Or my data wasn't clean enough.

00:07:17.130 --> 00:07:21.060
Or I need to add more features
or different features.

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:24.130
Or maybe I need a different
model class altogether.

00:07:24.130 --> 00:07:26.460
Anyway, you go through this
feature engineering cycle

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:29.190
or model development
cycle, and then what?

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:31.530
You end up with a model, right?

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:34.140
You have a model
laying on your desk.

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:35.820
Now what?

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:39.810
So training a model is
simply a means to an end.

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:41.670
It's not an end
in and of itself.

00:07:41.670 --> 00:07:44.740
We train models because we want
to do something interesting.

00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:47.190
The problem is-- let
me see how many of you

