00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:08.050
use the past seven days
to train a new model,

00:10:08.050 --> 00:10:09.020
and then go from there.

00:10:09.020 --> 00:10:09.520
OK?

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:15.000
This here is like
cheating, I guess.

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:16.740
Max samples is 7000.

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:18.365
Recall that I'm on a Pixelbook.

00:10:18.365 --> 00:10:19.740
I'm not going to
be able to train

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:21.240
on seven days worth
of data, and I'm

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:24.590
going to emphasize that point
on purpose a little bit later.

00:10:24.590 --> 00:10:27.044
This little beautiful
block of code

00:10:27.044 --> 00:10:28.210
is just reading in the data.

00:10:28.210 --> 00:10:30.940
And you you're like, why
are you using pandas?

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:33.190
I won't get into those
details, but there is a reason.

00:10:33.190 --> 00:10:34.950
It's to get the right
data format so we

00:10:34.950 --> 00:10:36.960
can use a dict vectorizer.

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:38.770
Here's the XGBoost regressor.

00:10:38.770 --> 00:10:44.370
I'm training a XGBoost
model, obviously.

00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:46.680
It's kind of like logistic
regression, I suppose.

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:48.877
And then we create an
sklearn pipeline where

00:10:48.877 --> 00:10:50.460
we have a dictionary
vectorizer, which

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:52.980
will turn the
categorical features,

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:56.590
do the one-hot encoding for us.

00:10:56.590 --> 00:11:02.230
I call pipeline.fit, we cross
our fingers that it works,

00:11:02.230 --> 00:11:04.710
and we won't necessarily
wait for it to finish,

00:11:04.710 --> 00:11:07.050
but it won't take very
long because I used so

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:08.650
little data and all that stuff.

00:11:08.650 --> 00:11:10.180
It says it's done.

00:11:10.180 --> 00:11:12.120
So with, essentially,
one line of code, you

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:12.930
save the model out.

00:11:12.930 --> 00:11:14.721
I'm sure you guys are
all familiar with how

00:11:14.721 --> 00:11:16.240
to pickle a model.

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:19.455
And then this command
here-- gsutil--

00:11:19.455 --> 00:11:21.420
it's just a file
copy command that you

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:24.345
use to go to the cloud, because
if you try to use cp, obviously

00:11:24.345 --> 00:11:26.580
it won't know how to
get it to the cloud.

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:27.660
So we save the model.

00:11:27.660 --> 00:11:29.340
We copied it to the cloud.

00:11:29.340 --> 00:11:32.700
Now we need to actually
deploy the model

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:34.140
as a service on the cloud.

00:11:34.140 --> 00:11:37.470
What we're going to do,
with these two commands

00:11:37.470 --> 00:11:41.040
here, is that's going to
create a REST API for you

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:43.122
that you can then send
prediction requests to.

00:11:43.122 --> 00:11:44.580
And it does all
the load balancing,

00:11:44.580 --> 00:11:47.910
it does all the auto-scaling,
it has the web server,

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:51.530
authentication, authorization,
prediction, et cetera.

00:11:51.530 --> 00:11:53.360
We'll talk more about
that, although I

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:55.940
might condense that part
of the slides, anyways,

00:11:55.940 --> 00:11:56.970
in a few minutes.

00:11:56.970 --> 00:11:59.090
So this first line
here, we have a notion

00:11:59.090 --> 00:12:02.210
in the current service called
a model, which is essentially

00:12:02.210 --> 00:12:04.740
a collection of versions.

00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:07.220
We'll see how this, in
the context of training

00:12:07.220 --> 00:12:11.590
new models every day, allows
you to keep various versions

00:12:11.590 --> 00:12:12.590
and switch between them.

00:12:12.590 --> 00:12:14.567
Like I'm serving
yesterday's model,

00:12:14.567 --> 00:12:17.150
and I now need to serve today's
model, but for the time being,

00:12:17.150 --> 00:12:18.525
think of it as a container.

00:12:18.525 --> 00:12:19.400
We create this model.

00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:21.022
If I do it again,
I'll get an error

00:12:21.022 --> 00:12:23.480
because they have to be unique,
so I'm not going to run it.

00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:26.060
Now this mess that is
cURL will not be there

00:12:26.060 --> 00:12:27.680
in about a week or two.

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:29.210
We just got the
command line tool

00:12:29.210 --> 00:12:32.250
updated to accept framework as
a command, and it wasn't there.

00:12:32.250 --> 00:12:35.600
So it's essentially very similar
to the model's create command,

00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:36.770
but it's versions create.

00:12:36.770 --> 00:12:39.150
So we're saying, create
a version of this model.

00:12:39.150 --> 00:12:42.710
And since I bumped up the
version to v4, when I run this,

00:12:42.710 --> 00:12:44.085
it's actually
going to the cloud.

00:12:44.085 --> 00:12:45.709
And you can imagine,
because it's doing

00:12:45.709 --> 00:12:47.120
all the things I talked about--

00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:50.094
load balancing, web servers,
blah, blah, blah, blah, blah,

00:12:50.094 --> 00:12:51.260
it does take a little while.

00:12:51.260 --> 00:12:53.210
But ours is actually very fast.

00:12:53.210 --> 00:12:57.107
It takes about-- I think the
median time is 90 seconds.

00:12:57.107 --> 00:12:59.440
Your very first model may
have a little bit more in it--

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:01.398
time, and be more on the
order of five minutes,

00:13:01.398 --> 00:13:04.440
but it takes about
a minute and a half.

00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:10.240
So while we're doing that, let's
switch back to the slide deck.

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:12.850
Now I know we have to do
