00:18:09.570 --> 00:18:12.660
So let's talk a little
bit about MLOps.

00:18:12.660 --> 00:18:15.200
This asterisk ops,
if you will, is sort

00:18:15.200 --> 00:18:16.450
of a buzzword in the industry.

00:18:16.450 --> 00:18:18.320
We've got DevOps
You've got DataOps.

00:18:18.320 --> 00:18:19.130
You've got PSYOPs.

00:18:19.130 --> 00:18:20.930
You've got-- I don't
know what other ops.

00:18:20.930 --> 00:18:25.297
So of course there's MLOps,
because why not, right?

00:18:25.297 --> 00:18:26.630
What do we mean by that, though?

00:18:26.630 --> 00:18:28.370
I like this diagram.

00:18:28.370 --> 00:18:31.820
If I hadn't stolen it from
a paper from some folks that

00:18:31.820 --> 00:18:33.380
work here at Google,
I probably would

00:18:33.380 --> 00:18:34.550
have chosen a little
different boxes,

00:18:34.550 --> 00:18:37.010
but I think it serves to
illustrate the point very, very

00:18:37.010 --> 00:18:38.962
well about what MLOps are.

00:18:38.962 --> 00:18:41.420
If I can direct your attention
to that teeny, teeny, teeny,

00:18:41.420 --> 00:18:44.750
teeny box in the center of the
screen, that's the ML code.

00:18:44.750 --> 00:18:47.720
That's the code that you guys
write as part of your job

00:18:47.720 --> 00:18:49.230
to get a model running.

00:18:49.230 --> 00:18:51.410
All of the other
stuff is what it

00:18:51.410 --> 00:18:53.660
takes to serve,
and not just serve,

00:18:53.660 --> 00:18:56.660
but to train and use ML
in a production scenario.

00:18:56.660 --> 00:18:58.160
Sure, if you're
just fooling around,

00:18:58.160 --> 00:18:59.600
like for a research
paper, and you

00:18:59.600 --> 00:19:02.600
got to get a more accurate
model than the last guy that

00:19:02.600 --> 00:19:04.020
published a paper
in this domain,

00:19:04.020 --> 00:19:05.270
you don't need all this stuff.

00:19:05.270 --> 00:19:07.019
You can do to do it
in a Jupyter notebook.

00:19:07.019 --> 00:19:09.459
Go use Colab, or Kaggle Kernels,
or your favorite Jupyter

00:19:09.459 --> 00:19:10.500
notebook and get it done.

00:19:10.500 --> 00:19:12.416
If you're going to do
something in production,

00:19:12.416 --> 00:19:14.179
there's a lot of work
that goes into it.

00:19:14.179 --> 00:19:16.220
You guys are probably very
familiar with the data

00:19:16.220 --> 00:19:18.770
collection phase, maybe
configuration as well.

00:19:18.770 --> 00:19:20.990
Obviously, feature extraction
in a production system

00:19:20.990 --> 00:19:23.115
that has to be running--
it's like a service that's

00:19:23.115 --> 00:19:24.720
running on a continual basis.

00:19:24.720 --> 00:19:26.220
We have your serving
infrastructure,

00:19:26.220 --> 00:19:28.636
which we already went into
slightly more details about how

00:19:28.636 --> 00:19:29.900
complex that can be.

00:19:29.900 --> 00:19:32.771
You've got to have resources
monitoring, blah, blah, blah,

00:19:32.771 --> 00:19:33.270
blah, blah.

00:19:33.270 --> 00:19:37.880
There's a lot that goes
into serving in production.

00:19:37.880 --> 00:19:40.220
When you define the term MLOps--

00:19:40.220 --> 00:19:44.090
when you define the
term DevOps, it's

00:19:44.090 --> 00:19:45.770
merging software
development processes

00:19:45.770 --> 00:19:48.830
with software operation.

00:19:48.830 --> 00:19:52.460
So I actually think that
that definition does not

00:19:52.460 --> 00:19:53.390
hold up for MLOps.

00:19:53.390 --> 00:19:54.080
Why?

00:19:54.080 --> 00:19:56.390
Because I feel like we
should be taking the ops out

00:19:56.390 --> 00:20:00.860
of ML, so that our jobs in
serving production systems

00:20:00.860 --> 00:20:03.200
is more like the small
black box, and less

00:20:03.200 --> 00:20:04.550
like everything around it.

00:20:04.550 --> 00:20:06.050
Let's let the
infrastructure-- let's

00:20:06.050 --> 00:20:07.341
let somebody else have the job.

00:20:07.341 --> 00:20:09.660
Let's let somebody else carry
the pager, type of thing.

00:20:09.660 --> 00:20:12.029
And so that's really our goal.

00:20:12.029 --> 00:20:13.820
Let me give you an
overview of what I think

00:20:13.820 --> 00:20:16.920
the steps should be for MLOps--

00:20:16.920 --> 00:20:19.580
with most of the ops
taken out of the MLOps.

00:20:19.580 --> 00:20:22.490
The first step is to
develop your model.

00:20:22.490 --> 00:20:27.911
I would hope that that
would be 99% of your time.

00:20:27.911 --> 00:20:30.410
You'd spend the three weeks to
get the best darned model you

00:20:30.410 --> 00:20:32.120
can out of your
data, and then you're

00:20:32.120 --> 00:20:33.950
ready to use it in production.

00:20:33.950 --> 00:20:35.570
Of course, this is
the nice GUI that I

00:20:35.570 --> 00:20:38.195
said that's not quite there yet,
but it will be in a few weeks.

00:20:38.195 --> 00:20:41.080
You click a few buttons
and you deploy it.

00:20:41.080 --> 00:20:43.310
You can go-- and I
forgot to do this.

00:20:43.310 --> 00:20:45.890
If we have a chance, we
can go back and look at it.

00:20:45.890 --> 00:20:49.704
But we have monitoring and
logging for all those models

00:20:49.704 --> 00:20:50.620
that we just deployed.

00:20:50.620 --> 00:20:51.730
Single-click, and
it's all there.

00:20:51.730 --> 00:20:52.729
So you're looking at it.

00:20:52.729 --> 00:20:55.270
You're looking at your
latencies and you say, oh, wait.

00:20:55.270 --> 00:20:57.660
Why is my latency so high?

00:20:57.660 --> 00:20:58.720
And then you say, oh.

00:20:58.720 --> 00:20:59.320
I get it.

00:20:59.320 --> 00:21:01.960
I didn't have enough
cores, or I didn't

00:21:01.960 --> 00:21:05.670
have enough nodes, to handle
the spikes that we had.

00:21:05.670 --> 00:21:07.420
You make a few tweaks,
you click a button,

00:21:07.420 --> 00:21:09.250
and you're off and going.

00:21:09.250 --> 00:21:11.575
And ideally, ideally you
don't even have to do this,

00:21:11.575 --> 00:21:13.450
but the reality is we're
not quite there yet,

00:21:13.450 --> 00:21:15.650
so you have to do a
little bit of tweaking.

00:21:15.650 --> 00:21:17.090
But you can even do it yourself.

00:21:17.090 --> 00:21:18.430
That's how easy it is.

00:21:18.430 --> 00:21:22.640
And that's how I think
MLOps should be done.

00:21:22.640 --> 00:21:23.990
So why cloud?

00:21:23.990 --> 00:21:25.900
Now I probably should've
asked a question.

00:21:25.900 --> 00:21:28.150
I don't know how many of
you-- well, let's just do it.

00:21:28.150 --> 00:21:31.000
How many of your
companies currently use

00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:33.550
the cloud for any of their ML?

00:21:33.550 --> 00:21:36.450
We have a few, but not many.

00:21:36.450 --> 00:21:39.904
And we find that to be true.

00:21:39.904 --> 00:21:41.320
I'm not here to
sell you on cloud.

00:21:41.320 --> 00:21:43.920
It might sound like it, and
I apologize if that's true.

00:21:43.920 --> 00:21:45.934
Obviously that's what
I do for a living,

00:21:45.934 --> 00:21:47.850
but let's just have an
open conversation here.

00:21:47.850 --> 00:21:49.630
The cloud isn't
necessarily for everybody.

00:21:49.630 --> 00:21:52.180
I had a few conversations
offline with some people.

00:21:52.180 --> 00:21:54.690
But here's where it does shine,
and if you fit these needs,

00:21:54.690 --> 00:21:56.251
you ought to consider it.

00:21:56.251 --> 00:21:58.500
We have world-class networking
and infrastructure here

00:21:58.500 --> 00:21:58.940
at Google.

00:21:58.940 --> 00:22:00.356
We really pride
ourselves on that.

00:22:00.356 --> 00:22:03.510
So you can count on lower
latencies, faster startup times

00:22:03.510 --> 00:22:06.270
and things than you'd get by
rolling your own solutions.

00:22:06.270 --> 00:22:09.200
Elasticity and scaling--
those are buzzwords as well,

00:22:09.200 --> 00:22:10.950
but if you think about
it, if you purchase

00:22:10.950 --> 00:22:13.200
a cluster of computers
to put in your own office

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:16.530
or your own data center,
you have a fixed cost.

00:22:16.530 --> 00:22:18.360
And if you use more
than that, you're

00:22:18.360 --> 00:22:19.742
going to get really
bad latencies

00:22:19.742 --> 00:22:22.200
when you're serving because
you don't have enough capacity.

00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:23.574
If you use less
than that, you're

00:22:23.574 --> 00:22:26.550
wasting money on servers that
are sitting idle, or worse,

00:22:26.550 --> 00:22:28.570
using power.

00:22:28.570 --> 00:22:31.170
And in the cloud, you're
paying for what you use.

00:22:31.170 --> 00:22:33.134
You scale up, you
pay for what you use.

00:22:33.134 --> 00:22:34.800
You scale down and
you're not using any,

00:22:34.800 --> 00:22:36.480
you don't pay for anything.

00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:37.241
High availability.

00:22:37.241 --> 00:22:39.240
Google prides itself on
this, and part of that's

00:22:39.240 --> 00:22:41.690
due to the next line,
which is Google SREs.

00:22:41.690 --> 00:22:43.890
SRE means site
reliability engineer.

00:22:43.890 --> 00:22:45.600
You may have heard of this.

00:22:45.600 --> 00:22:48.660
Google hires the
very best engineers

00:22:48.660 --> 00:22:52.140
that know how to run and
maintain production systems.

00:22:52.140 --> 00:22:54.720
And they helped
design the system so

00:22:54.720 --> 00:22:56.980
that there are fewer
outages to begin with,

00:22:56.980 --> 00:22:59.610
and when there are outages,
it's their pager that goes off.

00:22:59.610 --> 00:23:02.430
And they know how to
respond and quickly mitigate

00:23:02.430 --> 00:23:04.030
any problems that are there.

00:23:04.030 --> 00:23:06.314
And that's part of the
reason why Google Cloud has

00:23:06.314 --> 00:23:07.730
high availability
for its services

00:23:07.730 --> 00:23:09.870
is because of these folks here.

00:23:09.870 --> 00:23:10.800
Speed of deployment.

00:23:10.800 --> 00:23:13.920
You saw, in 90 seconds,
we went from a model

00:23:13.920 --> 00:23:16.200
that I trained on my
laptop to one that was

00:23:16.200 --> 00:23:18.910
serving at production grade.

00:23:18.910 --> 00:23:21.660
And then you'll
probably see this line--

00:23:21.660 --> 00:23:23.430
maybe I overemphasized
this, but I really

00:23:23.430 --> 00:23:26.280
believe that the total cost of
ownership and paying for what

00:23:26.280 --> 00:23:28.787
you use is a real
benefit of the cloud.

00:23:28.787 --> 00:23:30.120
So what if you're not ready yet?

00:23:30.120 --> 00:23:31.161
There's many reasons why.

00:23:31.161 --> 00:23:34.200
Maybe these use cases
doesn't fit your scenario.

00:23:34.200 --> 00:23:37.560
Maybe your company's really slow
at adopting things like cloud

00:23:37.560 --> 00:23:39.545
or other technologies.

00:23:39.545 --> 00:23:41.670
Or maybe you guys are just
onboarding but you still

00:23:41.670 --> 00:23:43.620
have a transition time.

00:23:43.620 --> 00:23:45.630
I'd like to refer
you to Kubeflow,

00:23:45.630 --> 00:23:49.440
which is built by our team--
the Cloud AI Platform--

00:23:49.440 --> 00:23:50.730
and what it is--

00:23:50.730 --> 00:23:53.310
I like to call it
the anaconda of ML.

00:23:53.310 --> 00:23:57.780
So it's a bunch of packages that
bring you ML, make ML very easy

00:23:57.780 --> 00:23:59.029
to do on Kubernetes.

00:23:59.029 --> 00:24:01.320
So if you guys are already
using Kubernetes or thinking

00:24:01.320 --> 00:24:03.120
about Kubernetes,
where you can run it

00:24:03.120 --> 00:24:07.920
on your laptop with Minikube,
where you can run it

00:24:07.920 --> 00:24:11.670
on-premise if you private
cloud or on-prem servers.

00:24:11.670 --> 00:24:14.440
You can run it on the cloud.

00:24:14.440 --> 00:24:16.732
Amazon just released
their Kubernetes.

00:24:16.732 --> 00:24:18.690
Microsoft has their
Kubernetes, but obviously I

00:24:18.690 --> 00:24:21.300
want you to use Google's
Kubernetes engine.

00:24:21.300 --> 00:24:24.270
And there's the hybrid
case where maybe you

00:24:24.270 --> 00:24:26.140
do have a data center
and it's at capacity,

00:24:26.140 --> 00:24:27.556
but when it goes
over capacity you

00:24:27.556 --> 00:24:29.220
can spill over to the cloud.

00:24:29.220 --> 00:24:30.870
And of course, the
cloud ML engine

00:24:30.870 --> 00:24:34.050
that I'm focusing on more
or less today that we have,

00:24:34.050 --> 00:24:37.055
I'd like to think of cloud ML
engine as managed Kubeflow.

00:24:37.055 --> 00:24:39.180
So if you don't want to
manage this stuff yourself,

00:24:39.180 --> 00:24:42.330
you can pay for the
managed service.

00:24:42.330 --> 00:24:42.930
All right.

00:24:42.930 --> 00:24:44.610
So I focused a
lot on prediction,

00:24:44.610 --> 00:24:46.830
but the MLOps stuff is
a lot more than just

00:24:46.830 --> 00:24:48.610
prediction, as we
saw in the diagram.

00:24:48.610 --> 00:24:52.860
So now I want to talk a little
bit about non-prediction cases.

00:24:52.860 --> 00:24:55.110
And there's a lot
I can say, and I

00:24:55.110 --> 00:24:58.660
can tell you, just as a teaser,
expect a lot from Google

00:24:58.660 --> 00:25:02.550
in the coming months, in a
lot of really cool stuff,

00:25:02.550 --> 00:25:04.500
and that's all I can say.

00:25:04.500 --> 00:25:08.010
But when might you want
to train in the cloud?

00:25:08.010 --> 00:25:09.630
These may seem
obvious, but let's take

00:25:09.630 --> 00:25:11.574
a second to talk about them.

00:25:11.574 --> 00:25:13.740
In the cloud you can get
really large machine types.

00:25:13.740 --> 00:25:17.370
How many of you guys have 96
cores sitting under your desk?

00:25:17.370 --> 00:25:20.010
If you do, I want your machine.

00:25:20.010 --> 00:25:21.930
I can't remember
what we cap out at,

00:25:21.930 --> 00:25:24.150
but I think it's somewhere
like a terabyte of RAM.

00:25:24.150 --> 00:25:26.850
We have machines that
have that much memory.

00:25:26.850 --> 00:25:28.726
And you know what I found?

00:25:28.726 --> 00:25:30.600
I actually had an intern
do this last summer.

00:25:30.600 --> 00:25:31.440
I said, hey.

00:25:31.440 --> 00:25:33.210
Go grab the Criteo data set.

00:25:33.210 --> 00:25:34.549
Go get as much RAM as you need.

00:25:34.549 --> 00:25:36.090
Get a VM with as
much RAM as you need

00:25:36.090 --> 00:25:37.548
and train it, and
see what happens.

00:25:37.548 --> 00:25:38.910
It took three hours to train.

00:25:38.910 --> 00:25:41.400
You needed a lot of RAM,
hundreds of gigabytes, I think.

00:25:41.400 --> 00:25:43.170
Took three hours to
train and he was done.

00:25:43.170 --> 00:25:45.990
He didn't have to go figure out
how to do distributed training.

00:25:45.990 --> 00:25:48.990
He just had to pay for three
hours of a server, a fairly

00:25:48.990 --> 00:25:50.820
reasonable price
with that much RAM,

00:25:50.820 --> 00:25:52.710
shut it down when
he was done, and he

00:25:52.710 --> 00:25:54.330
had a really great
model because it

00:25:54.330 --> 00:25:57.030
was trained on all that data.

00:25:57.030 --> 00:25:58.110
Number of cores.

00:25:58.110 --> 00:26:00.540
Imagine you're doing a
hyper-parameter sweep, grid

00:26:00.540 --> 00:26:02.790
search, or something
like that, and you

00:26:02.790 --> 00:26:04.260
want to do it in
parallel, but you

00:26:04.260 --> 00:26:05.801
don't want to set
up a SPARC cluster,

00:26:05.801 --> 00:26:07.830
or you don't want to
maintain any of that.

00:26:07.830 --> 00:26:10.080
Go get a machine with 96 cores.

00:26:10.080 --> 00:26:11.910
You can run 96--

00:26:11.910 --> 00:26:14.370
well, it depends on how many
cores each job's running,

00:26:14.370 --> 00:26:16.964
but you can do a lot of stuff
in parallel with 96 cores.

00:26:16.964 --> 00:26:18.630
Of course, you don't
want to go get a VM

00:26:18.630 --> 00:26:20.400
and do all your work on
that because you'd be

00:26:20.400 --> 00:26:21.608
paying for it the whole time.

00:26:21.608 --> 00:26:22.420
It's expensive.

00:26:22.420 --> 00:26:24.380
But this is what the
cloud brings to you.

00:26:24.380 --> 00:26:26.610
I talked about
distributed training.

00:26:26.610 --> 00:26:28.350
It is non-trivial.

00:26:28.350 --> 00:26:30.630
If anybody's had
to set up XGBoost

00:26:30.630 --> 00:26:34.370
to do parallel training
on SPARC, you can do it.

00:26:34.370 --> 00:26:36.600
There's some sample code
online to try and do it,

00:26:36.600 --> 00:26:38.950
but it's actually non-trivial.

00:26:38.950 --> 00:26:42.870
And we don't yet support
XGBoost distributed training,

00:26:42.870 --> 00:26:45.145
but for TensorFlow,
it's very simple

00:26:45.145 --> 00:26:46.270
to do distributed training.

00:26:46.270 --> 00:26:48.790
If you're using
tf.estimator, you literally

00:26:48.790 --> 00:26:50.500
have no code to change.

00:26:50.500 --> 00:26:52.510
You submit it to the
cloud and it scales up

00:26:52.510 --> 00:26:54.760
to the number of servers
you tell it to scale up to.

00:26:54.760 --> 00:26:56.980
And hopefully one day
we can bring something

00:26:56.980 --> 00:26:58.900
like that to XGBoost.

00:26:58.900 --> 00:27:00.550
We have a
hyper-parameter service

00:27:00.550 --> 00:27:03.390
called HyperTune, which
is available for XGBoost

00:27:03.390 --> 00:27:05.140
and scikit-learn models.

00:27:05.140 --> 00:27:08.860
And it gives you
much better results

00:27:08.860 --> 00:27:11.490
than doing a grid
search or random search.

00:27:11.490 --> 00:27:13.000
It's state-of-the-art
algorithms,

00:27:13.000 --> 00:27:15.140
so you'll get best-in-class
performance there,

00:27:15.140 --> 00:27:17.110
and it's all in parallel.

00:27:17.110 --> 00:27:19.141
You don't have to wait.

00:27:19.141 --> 00:27:20.890
So I talked to a
researcher here at Google

00:27:20.890 --> 00:27:22.720
and he says what he
does is he plays around

00:27:22.720 --> 00:27:24.136
during the day on
small data sets,

00:27:24.136 --> 00:27:26.590
gets things how he likes them,
sends off a job at night,

00:27:26.590 --> 00:27:28.090
comes back in the
morning and checks

00:27:28.090 --> 00:27:29.184
on how well his model did.

00:27:29.184 --> 00:27:31.600
Keeps his fingers crossed that
there was no errors halfway

00:27:31.600 --> 00:27:32.225
through, right?

00:27:32.225 --> 00:27:33.780
Can anybody relate to that?

00:27:33.780 --> 00:27:36.940
I know that's what I
did during my PhD, too.

00:27:36.940 --> 00:27:38.080
Repeatability.

00:27:38.080 --> 00:27:40.966
So what I mean by this is--

00:27:40.966 --> 00:27:43.090
there's more examples, but
the best example of this

00:27:43.090 --> 00:27:44.440
is with continuous training.

00:27:44.440 --> 00:27:47.110
So we're talking about
training a clickthrough rate

00:27:47.110 --> 00:27:49.690
model where you get new
logs, and tons of them,

00:27:49.690 --> 00:27:50.920
every single day.

00:27:50.920 --> 00:27:53.860
And every single day you're
going to train another model.

00:27:53.860 --> 00:27:55.630
And so you're going
to use the same code,

00:27:55.630 --> 00:27:57.463
and you're going to use
it every single day,

00:27:57.463 --> 00:28:00.590
or actually a lot of people
train like every hour,

00:28:00.590 --> 00:28:02.680
so they're training
very frequently.

00:28:02.680 --> 00:28:05.080
You're not going to want to
go to your Jupyter notebook

00:28:05.080 --> 00:28:07.870
and hit a button at
4:00 PM every day

00:28:07.870 --> 00:28:09.046
to train another model.

00:28:09.046 --> 00:28:10.420
This is a repeatable
process that

00:28:10.420 --> 00:28:11.544
needs to be productionized.

00:28:11.544 --> 00:28:13.150
It needs to be robust.

00:28:13.150 --> 00:28:15.610
And so the cloud is
really great for this.

00:28:15.610 --> 00:28:17.710
And in fact, if you're
taking advantage

00:28:17.710 --> 00:28:20.320
of those large machines
and those large cores,

00:28:20.320 --> 00:28:21.314
it sends off a job.

00:28:21.314 --> 00:28:23.480
It uses all those things
for a short period of time.

00:28:23.480 --> 00:28:25.390
It shuts it down, and you
only pay for that time

00:28:25.390 --> 00:28:26.265
that you're doing it.

00:28:30.630 --> 00:28:33.600
So let's come back
here to see what that

00:28:33.600 --> 00:28:36.320
might look like in the cloud.

00:28:36.320 --> 00:28:38.600
So there is a price
to pay when you're

00:28:38.600 --> 00:28:41.417
switching from your local
machine to the cloud.

00:28:41.417 --> 00:28:43.250
You do need to package
up your code in a way

00:28:43.250 --> 00:28:44.330
that the service can understand.

00:28:44.330 --> 00:28:45.570
And there are multiple
ways to do that.

00:28:45.570 --> 00:28:47.111
We'll be supporting
containers, which

00:28:47.111 --> 00:28:49.250
is becoming an
industry-standard way to do it.

00:28:49.250 --> 00:28:51.990
For now, we use Python packages.

00:28:51.990 --> 00:28:57.920
So you just run
python setup.py sdist.

00:28:57.920 --> 00:28:59.990
You see the second
command then uploads it

00:28:59.990 --> 00:29:02.160
to a bucket in the cloud.

00:29:02.160 --> 00:29:05.270
And I think that's finished.

00:29:05.270 --> 00:29:09.700
And then I'll go
ahead and hit Enter,

00:29:09.700 --> 00:29:12.210
and then you can see right
here all I'm doing is saying,

00:29:12.210 --> 00:29:15.160
submit a training job called
CTR, for clickthrough rate,

00:29:15.160 --> 00:29:17.260
with the time stamp
so it's unique.

00:29:17.260 --> 00:29:19.980
I'm saying that
it's trainer.train.

00:29:19.980 --> 00:29:22.030
This package is what
we just uploaded.

00:29:22.030 --> 00:29:24.310
The runtime version tells
us which version of--

00:29:24.310 --> 00:29:27.310
now I wish that was
more obvious that this

00:29:27.310 --> 00:29:28.960
was like scikit-learn
whatever version,

00:29:28.960 --> 00:29:32.360
but this does translate
into scikit-learn version.

00:29:32.360 --> 00:29:36.022
This is Cloud ML Engine's
runtime version 1.8.

00:29:36.022 --> 00:29:37.480
We're going to do
it in US central.

00:29:37.480 --> 00:29:40.060
It's with this project, and a
few command-line parameters.

00:29:40.060 --> 00:29:42.280
So just since I
forgot to say this--

00:29:42.280 --> 00:29:46.390
this train.py is that exact
code we saw at the beginning.

00:29:46.390 --> 00:29:48.850
The only thing is you
would probably take off

00:29:48.850 --> 00:29:51.160
that max sample
lines that I had,

00:29:51.160 --> 00:29:52.960
because my Pixelbook
couldn't handle it,

00:29:52.960 --> 00:29:55.085
and I won't admit how long
it took me to figure out

00:29:55.085 --> 00:29:57.010
that's what was
crashing on my notebook

00:29:57.010 --> 00:29:58.420
because it didn't tell me.

00:29:58.420 --> 00:30:00.490
And you know, you might
change the max depth,

00:30:00.490 --> 00:30:02.952
or the number of
estimators to be what

00:30:02.952 --> 00:30:04.160
you would have in production.

00:30:04.160 --> 00:30:05.660
This is your actual
production code.

00:30:05.660 --> 00:30:06.910
So you make a few tweaks.

00:30:06.910 --> 00:30:08.470
You send it off to the cloud.

00:30:08.470 --> 00:30:09.970
You can see that
the job was queued.

00:30:09.970 --> 00:30:12.020
Again, we don't have a
first-class integration,

00:30:12.020 --> 00:30:17.530
so we have to go over
here to our list of jobs.

00:30:17.530 --> 00:30:18.130
And let's see.

00:30:18.130 --> 00:30:19.720
That one-- you see
the spinny thing

00:30:19.720 --> 00:30:22.310
means it's still
spinning up the machines.

00:30:22.310 --> 00:30:25.840
Our prediction service
actually spins up

00:30:25.840 --> 00:30:27.290
the machines very, very fast.

00:30:27.290 --> 00:30:29.060
The training takes
a little bit longer.

00:30:29.060 --> 00:30:30.601
I think if it's a
single-machine job,

00:30:30.601 --> 00:30:32.960
it's usually around
one or two minutes.

00:30:32.960 --> 00:30:37.270
So we can go to this job
here, take a look at the logs.

00:30:37.270 --> 00:30:39.310
Nothing super interesting,
just to show you

00:30:39.310 --> 00:30:42.130
that there's this integration
here with the logs

00:30:42.130 --> 00:30:47.081
if I'm lucky enough
to get it to come up.

00:30:47.081 --> 00:30:48.580
So you can see that
all the output's

00:30:48.580 --> 00:30:50.500
captured in the logs in
case you need to go back

00:30:50.500 --> 00:30:52.041
and figure out
something that's wrong

00:30:52.041 --> 00:30:53.760
or something that's right.

00:30:53.760 --> 00:30:57.980
And you can see that the
job completed successfully.

00:30:57.980 --> 00:31:00.230
So that's how you
send off a single job.

00:31:00.230 --> 00:31:02.230
But the use case that
I was talking about

00:31:02.230 --> 00:31:04.510
is really about productionizing
this and doing it

00:31:04.510 --> 00:31:06.880
on a daily basis.

00:31:06.880 --> 00:31:09.917
How many of you are familiar
with Airflow, by chance?

00:31:09.917 --> 00:31:10.750
A couple of you are?

00:31:10.750 --> 00:31:12.525
Probably the data
engineers, I think.

00:31:12.525 --> 00:31:13.900
I mentioned to
somebody before we

00:31:13.900 --> 00:31:16.910
started I'd hint at a
little bit of pipeline here.

00:31:16.910 --> 00:31:19.860
So what Airflow is-- and there's
lots of orchestration systems,

00:31:19.860 --> 00:31:22.690
but it allows you to
chain together various

00:31:22.690 --> 00:31:26.350
processes that you can then
do on a repeated basis.

00:31:26.350 --> 00:31:29.170
In this case, on a daily basis.

00:31:29.170 --> 00:31:30.640
You can see that right here.

00:31:30.640 --> 00:31:32.510
And I'll get into what
this actually means,

00:31:32.510 --> 00:31:34.480
but you can see that
every day for the last two

00:31:34.480 --> 00:31:38.020
weeks, this code that we
just submitted to the service

00:31:38.020 --> 00:31:41.260
to run to train a model has
run on the previous seven

00:31:41.260 --> 00:31:44.050
days worth of data,
produced a model,

00:31:44.050 --> 00:31:46.480
and then pushed that
model to serving.

00:31:46.480 --> 00:31:49.900
So let's go ahead and you can
see that in a graph view here.

00:31:49.900 --> 00:31:51.400
Very, very simple DAG.

00:31:51.400 --> 00:31:53.470
I did that for the
sake of this demo.

00:31:53.470 --> 00:31:56.530
It just does training and create
version, but some of you guys

00:31:56.530 --> 00:31:58.926
have already pointed out an
obvious problem with that.

00:31:58.926 --> 00:32:00.550
If something went
wrong during training

00:32:00.550 --> 00:32:03.460
and I pushed it to production,
I'd be in a world of hurt.

00:32:03.460 --> 00:32:05.020
Could even get fired for that.

00:32:05.020 --> 00:32:07.540
So what you would normally
do, and it's actually not

00:32:07.540 --> 00:32:08.220
that difficult.

00:32:08.220 --> 00:32:11.140
You can put another mode in
the graph that runs evaluation,

00:32:11.140 --> 00:32:12.610
and another node
in the graph that

00:32:12.610 --> 00:32:15.220
checks that the
quality of that model

00:32:15.220 --> 00:32:17.860
is better than the previous,
or the model that's currently

00:32:17.860 --> 00:32:20.382
serving, before you push
it, and then go ahead

00:32:20.382 --> 00:32:21.340
and push it to serving.

00:32:21.340 --> 00:32:25.600
But for simplicity's sake,
I just trained and pushed.

00:32:25.600 --> 00:32:28.690
And you can look at-- the
code to do that is actually

00:32:28.690 --> 00:32:30.380
very, very reasonable here.

00:32:30.380 --> 00:32:33.260
Again, you have the preliminary,
which binary do I use,

00:32:33.260 --> 00:32:34.840
et cetera.

00:32:34.840 --> 00:32:37.819
These DAG args are just
necessary for Airflow to work.

00:32:37.819 --> 00:32:38.860
A little bit of overhead.

00:32:38.860 --> 00:32:40.967
Copy and paste is what I
like to think of it as.

00:32:40.967 --> 00:32:43.550
And then here's where you say,
hey, with DAG-- in other words,

00:32:43.550 --> 00:32:45.880
with my pipeline that
I'm trying to define--

00:32:45.880 --> 00:32:47.470
you have this training
op which looks

00:32:47.470 --> 00:32:50.860
very close to the gcloud
operation, but in Python code.

00:32:50.860 --> 00:32:54.174
It says hey, use this
binary for training.

00:32:54.174 --> 00:32:55.840
And then you have the
create version op,

00:32:55.840 --> 00:32:58.006
which again looks like the
gcloud option for pushing

00:32:58.006 --> 00:32:58.940
a version.

00:32:58.940 --> 00:33:01.420
So I mean I don't know how
many lines of code that is,

00:33:01.420 --> 00:33:03.180
but the core of this is, what?

00:33:03.180 --> 00:33:05.320
20 lines of code, maybe?

00:33:05.320 --> 00:33:08.350
And we've defined this
pipeline that we're

00:33:08.350 --> 00:33:14.810
able to run in the cloud on
a daily basis that pushes

00:33:14.810 --> 00:33:16.010
the models to production.

00:33:16.010 --> 00:33:17.540
Let's see if I have this.

00:33:17.540 --> 00:33:20.930
So you can see, here's
the model ctrdaily.

00:33:20.930 --> 00:33:23.480
Remember how I said, model's
like a container for versions?

00:33:23.480 --> 00:33:25.490
And here are all the
versions of that model that

00:33:25.490 --> 00:33:26.697
got pushed on a daily basis.

00:33:26.697 --> 00:33:28.280
Now also on a
production system, you'd

00:33:28.280 --> 00:33:29.990
probably be
garbage-collecting these

00:33:29.990 --> 00:33:32.690
so you don't have so
many laying around.

00:33:32.690 --> 00:33:34.850
You can see that this
is the default one,

00:33:34.850 --> 00:33:36.370
so if I send traffic
to this model,

00:33:36.370 --> 00:33:38.120
it's going to actually
go to that version.

00:33:38.120 --> 00:33:41.510
You'd want to update
that on a daily basis,

00:33:41.510 --> 00:33:45.280
at least after
you'd validated it.

00:33:45.280 --> 00:33:47.960
So yeah.

00:33:47.960 --> 00:33:48.990
Any questions?

00:33:48.990 --> 00:33:49.890
I forgot to say this.

00:33:49.890 --> 00:33:52.357
It was kind of difficult with
all the interruptions we had.

00:33:52.357 --> 00:33:54.690
My preference was going to
be to have questions in-line,

00:33:54.690 --> 00:33:56.880
but I'm almost at the
end of my talk now.

00:33:56.880 --> 00:34:01.000
But any questions about these
pipelines or the training?

00:34:01.000 --> 00:34:01.500
Yeah.

00:34:01.500 --> 00:34:03.460
AUDIENCE: The logging
that you showed--

00:34:03.460 --> 00:34:03.700
ROBBIE HAERTEL: Yeah.

00:34:03.700 --> 00:34:05.688
AUDIENCE: Is that something
that spit out of the cloud

00:34:05.688 --> 00:34:07.344
box itself, or is
that something that

00:34:07.344 --> 00:34:10.670
if I had logging in my code,
that I can spit out to?

00:34:10.670 --> 00:34:11.969
ROBBIE HAERTEL: Yep.

00:34:11.969 --> 00:34:12.659
Yeah.

00:34:12.659 --> 00:34:15.840
So if you do your print
statements-- your logging.info

00:34:15.840 --> 00:34:17.699
statements, if your
Python programmer--

00:34:17.699 --> 00:34:19.510
those things show up here.

00:34:19.510 --> 00:34:22.290
And I think it tells
you which one's which.

00:34:22.290 --> 00:34:25.770
The standard error
shows up unfortunately,

00:34:25.770 --> 00:34:29.429
as orange warning signs.

00:34:29.429 --> 00:34:30.929
There's nothing to
be worried about.

00:34:30.929 --> 00:34:36.130
That's just XGBoost telling
us that it's doing work.

00:34:36.130 --> 00:34:37.415
Any other questions?

00:34:41.870 --> 00:34:43.909
So this is really
the end of my talk.

00:34:43.909 --> 00:34:46.040
Just to summarize
in a few points,

00:34:46.040 --> 00:34:47.870
productionization is hard.

00:34:47.870 --> 00:34:49.199
Cloud is easy.

00:34:49.199 --> 00:34:52.489
And you pay for what
you use on the cloud.

00:34:52.489 --> 00:34:53.480
All right.

00:34:53.480 --> 00:34:55.190
And a few teasers,
I guess, before we

00:34:55.190 --> 00:34:56.630
move to see if there's
any other questions

00:34:56.630 --> 00:34:57.980
about the entirety of the talk.

00:34:57.980 --> 00:35:02.420
But our team is working
on increased focus

00:35:02.420 --> 00:35:03.620
on reducing MLOps.

00:35:03.620 --> 00:35:05.930
So we saw probably 10
boxes on that page,

00:35:05.930 --> 00:35:09.920
and I addressed a few of them,
like the continuous training

00:35:09.920 --> 00:35:11.240
case and the serving case.

00:35:11.240 --> 00:35:13.340
But there's actually a
lot more boxes on there,

00:35:13.340 --> 00:35:14.840
and you better
believe that we're

00:35:14.840 --> 00:35:19.610
spending time making this easier
and easier as time goes on.

00:35:19.610 --> 00:35:22.340
Increased cost
efficiencies work.

00:35:22.340 --> 00:35:25.700
That's a continuous focus
for us because we're

00:35:25.700 --> 00:35:28.460
able to amortize the cost
of the infrastructure

00:35:28.460 --> 00:35:29.250
across everybody.

00:35:29.250 --> 00:35:31.160
So we're actually able
to get you lower costs

00:35:31.160 --> 00:35:32.840
than you might on your own.

00:35:32.840 --> 00:35:34.370
And then we're also
working to allow

00:35:34.370 --> 00:35:37.460
data scientists like yourself
and even data engineers,

00:35:37.460 --> 00:35:41.670
as well, to share their
reusable work with others.

00:35:41.670 --> 00:35:43.160
We're working on
ways to do that.

